A||N||10||Jonathan||My fiero feels like a boat!||||9||
Z||000000||Jonathan||12-14-2000||09:25 AM||||My Fiero is finally out of the body shop, and I have started to drive it again.  It has been almost eleven months since I drove it with any regularity.  Now it seems that the suspension is completely whipped.  I know the shocks, and struts are shot.  I also feel that the original stock bushings are mostly worn out.  I know the engine mounts are fairly worn.  I don't remember the car being this bad when I started working on it.  <P>I know that poly bushings will make a great improvement, but...  I wonder what it would feel like with new KYBs all around, and new OEM bushings?  From what I can gather the great improvement that everyone sees from poly is compared to their old worn out bushings.  Does anyone know how well the poly compares to the OEM rubber when new?  I don't mind putting in poly.  However, I can't stand poly chirp.  I know they can be greased, but wonder how long that lasts.  (It only takes one bushing to start chirping to really annoy me!)<P>Any thoughts or ideas?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fierotag2.jpg"> ||||reg||9
Z||000001||Patrick's Dad||12-14-2000||09:46 AM||||I can't answer to the bushings yet. My Formula had stock bushings all around, and I won't get to disassembling the cradle until the summer, and the suspension possibly until 2002....<P>I had KYB's and lowering springs (Non-named, from the Fiero Store) installed a couple of years ago. The KYB's are progressive rate shocks. I found them very comfortable on the open road, yet I could throw out the tail almost at will (Mostly stock engine, 225/60's on rear). I wanted Koni's but my wife said less $$. I wasn't unhappy with the KYB's at all.<P>From what I've read, poly bushings, especially on the engine cradle, result in less engine movement, therefore quicker response, but no better actual performance. The tradeoff is harshness, as the poly transmit more NVH through the frame of the car. This won't bug me, but you may want to consider this, based on your post.<P>I'm trying to type coherent sentences through "Bananas in Pajamas," which my kids are watching....<P>------------------<BR>This should work until my site comes online....   <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/Tmh.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-14-2000||11:36 AM||||I like my KYB's. I rode in a car that had the KONI's (set to firmest) and I thought it was a little too harsh for daily driving. The poly's feel great. The chirping is hard to get rid of. I greased mine very very well and normally they don't chirp except when going over dips or speed bumps slowly or when it is cold (they chirp for a mile or so until they "warm-up"). Cradle poly bushings usually don't chirp if greased and they don't affect ride too much. They just add a little more vibration, But even a 4cyl will feel a little more peppy since the cradle flex is less.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||12-14-2000||03:27 PM||||I have KYB all around on my Mercedes SL and Bilstein on my Fiero. Im happy with handleing in both. Dont know about bushings yet, ill decide on those when I need them. My Fiero doesnt have any body roll in hard corning, so Im not even bothering with any swaybars.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Got2manyfieros||12-14-2000||06:43 PM||||I Switched from KYB's to Koni's and there is NO COMPARISSON, the koni's are amazing, and it is nice that you can loosen them up when you are just going for a quick cruise, and tighten them way down if you autocross or take part in other types of competition.  Also, the polyurethane bushings are about 20 percent stiffer than factory rubber bushings.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MrPBody||12-14-2000||07:04 PM||||IRM sells stiffer rubber bushings.  I'm hoping someone here will install them, and tell us how they work.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jonathan||12-15-2000||10:55 AM||||Do you have the link for the IRM rubber bushing?||||reg||1
Z||000007||MrPBody||12-15-2000||05:46 PM||||IRM does not have a Web site; however, FieroMaster has scanned an out-of-date catalog<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Lane/1588/quickcat.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Lane/1588/quickcat.html</A> <P>I think IRM's e-mail is IRMFIERO@aol.com.  They will send a free paper catalog.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bob Englert||12-15-2000||08:57 PM||||Jonathan:<P>Hoop here in Greensboro just rebuilt his entire front and rear suspension with poly bushings,poly cradle bushings, lowering saprings and KYB shocks.  If you ever have a chance to drive over this way we can all get together and Hoop would probably love to take you for a ride in his 86GT (and show off!).<P>I just installed new rubber bushings and new Monroe shopcks in my blue 87 GT.  Everything is tight but not as stiff as the poly will be in Hoop's car.  You could probably get a good comparision between our cars.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000009||WisconsinGT||12-16-2000||11:06 PM||||I went too IRM's site. I did not see any bushings for the 88GT. Anyone know where I can get a non-urethane bushing set that is better then stock?||||reg||1
Z||000010||rodmcneill||12-17-2000||12:39 AM||||I saw recently a company is working on delrin bushings for the Fiero suspension. I dont think they will squeak but delrin is a harder substance than poly. so these will ride even stiffer if they make it to production.||||reg||1
